in this episode of things I learned  about loud spanner we look at right  patching to help a database specialist  accelerate their data load will we find  the right strategies for higher  throughputs stay tuned to find out in  the previous episode I helped my friend  Anna and her company pile of gadgets and  online shop for gadget friends we were  able to solve a hot spotting issue  during their data load that kept the  throughput low and that loud spanner  instance underutilized by choosing new  well distributed primary keys for the  tables we saw an increase in throughput  and utilization now we are going to see  if we can improve the data load  performance even further the data load  app which reads records from CSV files  and writes them to cloud spanner was  writing only one row per transaction  since our goal is to load data as fast  possible loading with a single row per  transaction doesn't sound like the  optimal solution it seemed obvious to  batch as many rights as possible  together to see a conservable throughput  boost while staying below transaction  limits of course the records we were  loading are fairly small so we increase  the batch size to a thousand rows per  transaction and expect that much higher  throughputs due to the reduced line  server communications and transactional  wed surprisingly we only saw a bit more  than doubling of our rights to put fifty  thousand rows instead of the previous 20  thousand rows per second don't get me  wrong more than doubling the support is  great but we wanted to see if we can  improve this even further as it turns  out we were still missing a step to get  the real benefits of batching the roll  set were patched together have well  distributed primary Keys the ones we  introduced in the previous episode to  fix the hot spotting issue during data  load that means writing 1000 of those  rows with well distributed primary keys  in a single transaction ends up  involving many many table splits as you  know a table is split based on size and  load patterns in ranges of rows ordered  alphabetically by primary key the  responsibility for this split is then  distributed among the available compute  nodes where each split is controlled by  exactly one node now when we write to  multiple splits in a single transaction  cloud span on  to coordinate between all the nodes that  are responsible for these splits for  that it needs to obtain locks and CLI  say access for all outstanding  transactions writing to any of these  splits using the two-phase commit  protocol  this means the benefits of having  multiple rolls in one transaction are  reduced by coordination overhead and  cured by spanning multiple splits armed  with this knowledge of the inner  workings of cloud spanner we needed a  way to reduce the number of splits  accessed by each transaction to gain the  maximum benefit of batching we went back  to our Lauria program and modified it to  load a large number of rows into cash we  then sorted these rows alphabetically by  primary key the same way the table  splits are ordered and partition them by  our chosen batch size from there we  created one transaction for each of the  partitions some might ask here  then we just introduced math distributed  primary keys to avoid hot spotting in  the previous episode and now we are  sorting right the trick here is that the  ordered petitions are distinct in the  primary key ranges they write to  spending fewer table splits and sewers  causing less contention during writes as  you can see in the graph this improves  our load performance significantly to  about 120,000 rows per second a six-fold  increase to very start from now to find  the optimal load performance we have to  keep a couple of limitations in mind  besides optimal size ranges for  transactions between 1 to 10 megabytes  there is a strict limitation of 20,000  mutation per transaction mutation what  is that a mutation has a special meaning  here where any modification of a single  cell in the table or index accounts for  one mutation except if you delete a  range of rows so for example if you have  a table with five columns and an index  defined on three of those columns  including the primary key columns each  insert of a row accounts for 8 notations  now if you don't want to write all these  logic into your own data loader you can  use the cloud dataflow connector for  cloud spanner the connector does Soaring  partitioning and scaling out writes  automatically for you  check out the sample source code we  linked in the description to learn more  about it after all these data load  optimizations my friends company pile of  caches was now happy with the load  performance and started to look into how  to optimize apps for scalability and  performance unfortunately we are out of  time in this episode so stay tuned for  our next one where we show how to design  our transactions for scalability and  performance  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

